1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,296
This is MATH 239, video 18a. It works through problems 1 to 5 of the Practice Midterm.

2
00:00:08,746 --> 00:00:24,814
After this video, you can prove an identity by counting one set in two ways. You can define a bijection and its inverse. You can count compositions with conditions. You can decide if a weight function gives a formal power series. And you can sum a finite geometric series.

3
00:00:25,264 --> 00:00:37,013
The Practice Midterm has 10 problems. Together they are worth 79 marks. The real midterm has about 50 marks. The practice paper has more problems, so that it covers all topics.

4
00:00:37,463 --> 00:00:49,863
This video does problems 1 to 5. They use chapters 1 and 2 of the notes: counting, bijections, compositions, and generating series. Video 18b does problems 6 to 10.

5
00:00:50,313 --> 00:00:58,858
You can use all results from class without proof. Name each result that you use, for example the Product Lemma. And justify every answer.

6
00:00:59,308 --> 00:01:04,044
Before each solution, pause the video and try the problem on paper first.

7
00:01:04,494 --> 00:01:18,844
Problem 1 is worth 10 marks. Let k and n be integers with k between 0 and n. S is the set of ordered pairs A, B. A and B are subsets of 1 to n, and each has exactly k elements.

8
00:01:19,294 --> 00:01:30,161
Here is one element of S for n equal to 4 and k equal to 2. A is the set 1, 2. B is the set 2, 4. A and B can share elements. Here they share the element 2.

9
00:01:30,611 --> 00:01:45,983
We must prove that n choose k, squared, equals the sum over i from 0 to k of n choose i, times n minus i choose k minus i, times n minus k choose k minus i. The method is given: count S in two different ways.

10
00:01:46,433 --> 00:01:53,120
The hint says: look at the possible values of the size of A intersect B. Pause the video and try it.

11
00:01:53,570 --> 00:02:09,360
First count. To build a pair, choose A first. A is a k-element subset of an n-element set. By Theorem 1.5 there are n choose k choices. For n equal to 4 and k equal to 2, there are 6 choices: the six rows.

12
00:02:09,810 --> 00:02:17,380
Then choose B. The choices for B do not depend on A. So B also has n choose k choices. These are the six columns.

13
00:02:17,830 --> 00:02:23,681
Each pair A, B is one cell of the grid. So the grid has 6 times 6 cells, which is 36.

14
00:02:24,131 --> 00:02:34,208
In general, the number of pairs is n choose k, times n choose k. So the size of S is n choose k, squared. This is the left side.

15
00:02:34,658 --> 00:02:44,504
Second count. Let S sub i be the set of pairs with exactly i common elements, for i from 0 to k. These sets are disjoint, and their union is S.

16
00:02:44,954 --> 00:03:03,297
Fix i. We build a pair in S sub i with three choices. First, choose A intersect B. It is an i-element subset of 1 to n, so there are n choose i choices. In the example, n is 7, k is 3, and i is 1. We choose the element 3.

17
00:03:03,747 --> 00:03:21,441
Second, choose the rest of A, that is, A minus B. It has k minus i elements. They come from the n minus i elements that are not in A intersect B. There are n minus i choose k minus i choices. Here we choose 1 and 5. Now A is complete.

18
00:03:21,891 --> 00:03:40,049
Third, choose B minus A. It also has k minus i elements. They must not be in A. A has k elements, so n minus k elements are left. There are n minus k choose k minus i choices. Here we choose 2 and 6 from 2, 4, 6 and 7.

19
00:03:40,499 --> 00:03:55,638
The three choices give each pair in S sub i exactly once. So the size of S sub i is the product of the three binomial coefficients. Add over i from 0 to k. This gives the right side. Both sides count S, so they are equal.

20
00:03:56,088 --> 00:04:03,147
Check the identity with n equal to 4 and k equal to 2. Each cell of the grid now shows i, the size of A intersect B.

21
00:04:03,597 --> 00:04:14,418
i equal to 2 means that A equals B. These are the 6 cells on the diagonal. The formula gives 4 choose 2, times 2 choose 0, times 2 choose 0. That is 6.

22
00:04:14,868 --> 00:04:27,732
i equal to 1: A and B share one element. The formula gives 4 choose 1, times 3 choose 1, times 2 choose 1. That is 4 times 3 times 2, which is 24. The grid has 24 orange cells.

23
00:04:28,182 --> 00:04:35,426
i equal to 0: A and B are disjoint. The formula gives 1, times 4 choose 2, times 2 choose 2. That is 6.

24
00:04:35,876 --> 00:04:43,632
6 plus 24 plus 6 is 36, which is 6 squared. A python check confirms the identity for all n up to 7.

25
00:04:44,082 --> 00:04:57,131
Here is the written solution. Count 1: there are n choose k choices for A, and independently n choose k choices for B. Cite Theorem 1.5. So the size of S is n choose k, squared.

26
00:04:57,581 --> 00:05:09,609
Count 2: define S sub i for each i from 0 to k. Write why the S sub i are disjoint and why their union is S. The size of A intersect B is always between 0 and k.

27
00:05:10,059 --> 00:05:18,879
Then give the three choices with their numbers. Say that each pair arises exactly once. This sentence shows that the product is the size of S sub i.

28
00:05:19,329 --> 00:05:26,853
Add the sizes. Both counts give the size of S, so the two expressions are equal. End the proof with this sentence.

29
00:05:27,303 --> 00:05:38,495
A common mistake is in the third factor. Some students choose B minus A from the n minus i elements outside A intersect B. That pool still contains A minus B.

30
00:05:38,945 --> 00:05:52,459
Then B can contain the element 1, which is in A. Now 1 is in A intersect B, and the intersection has 2 elements, not i. The pool for B minus A must avoid all of A. So it has n minus k elements.

31
00:05:52,909 --> 00:06:12,460
Also, some terms of the sum are 0. For example, take n equal to 5, k equal to 3, and i equal to 0. The term has 2 choose 3, which is 0. Two 3-element subsets of a 5-element set always share an element. The formula gives 0 for this case, so no special case is necessary.

32
00:06:12,910 --> 00:06:23,266
Problem 2 is worth 10 marks. Let n be at least 3. A sub n is the set of 3-element subsets of 1 to n. For n equal to 5, there are 10 such subsets.

33
00:06:23,716 --> 00:06:34,351
B sub n is the set of triples b 1, b 2, b 3 of positive integers whose sum is at most n. For n equal to 5, these are the 10 triples on the right.

34
00:06:34,801 --> 00:06:47,154
We must define a bijection f from A sub n to B sub n, and show where f sends each element of A sub 5. Then show that f of S is in B sub n, and state the inverse. Other details are not necessary.

35
00:06:47,604 --> 00:06:48,904
Pause the video and try it.

36
00:06:49,354 --> 00:07:00,175
Write the elements of S in increasing order: a less than b less than c. In the example, n is 9 and S is the set 2, 5, 6.

37
00:07:00,625 --> 00:07:11,421
Cut the row 1 to n after a, after b, and after c. The first piece is 1 to a. It has a cells. Let b 1 equal a. Here b 1 is 2.

38
00:07:11,871 --> 00:07:20,572
The second piece is a plus 1 to b. It has b minus a cells. Let b 2 equal b minus a. Here b 2 is 3.

39
00:07:21,022 --> 00:07:34,583
The third piece is b plus 1 to c. It has c minus b cells. Let b 3 equal c minus b. Here b 3 is 1. Each piece ends at an element of S, so each piece has at least one cell.

40
00:07:35,033 --> 00:07:51,890
The rest of the row, c plus 1 to n, is not used. So b 1 plus b 2 plus b 3 equals c, which is at most n. Define f of a, b, c as the triple a, b minus a, c minus b. Here f gives 2, 3, 1.

41
00:07:52,340 --> 00:08:05,976
Now apply f to the 10 elements of A sub 5. 1, 2, 3 goes to 1, 1, 1. 1, 2, 4 goes to 1, 1, 2. 1, 2, 5 goes to 1, 1, 3.

42
00:08:06,426 --> 00:08:12,742
1, 3, 4 goes to 1, 2, 1. 1, 3, 5 goes to 1, 2, 2. 1, 4, 5 goes to 1, 3, 1.

43
00:08:13,192 --> 00:08:24,987
2, 3, 4 goes to 2, 1, 1. 2, 3, 5 goes to 2, 1, 2. 2, 4, 5 goes to 2, 2, 1. And 3, 4, 5 goes to 3, 1, 1.

44
00:08:25,437 --> 00:08:35,840
Each triple of B sub 5 gets exactly one arrow. So for n equal to 5, f is a bijection. The question asks for this table, so write all 10 lines.

45
00:08:36,290 --> 00:08:47,993
We must show that f of S is in B sub n for every S in A sub n. Let S be a, b, c, with 1 at most a, a less than b, b less than c, and c at most n.

46
00:08:48,443 --> 00:08:53,133
b 1 equals a. Since a is at least 1, b 1 is at least 1.

47
00:08:53,583 --> 00:09:07,887
b 2 equals b minus a. b is larger than a, and both are integers. So b 2 is at least 1. In the same way, b 3 equals c minus b, and it is at least 1.

48
00:09:08,337 --> 00:09:20,922
The sum b 1 plus b 2 plus b 3 is a, plus b minus a, plus c minus b. The terms cancel, and the sum is c. And c is at most n. All conditions hold, so f of S is in B sub n.

49
00:09:21,372 --> 00:09:34,951
The inverse undoes the cuts. Take a triple b 1, b 2, b 3 in B sub n. Put down blocks of lengths b 1, b 2 and b 3, from the left. Here the triple is 1, 3, 1.

50
00:09:35,401 --> 00:09:49,705
The blocks end at b 1, at b 1 plus b 2, and at b 1 plus b 2 plus b 3. Define f inverse of the triple as the set of these three numbers. For 1, 3, 1 we get 1, 4, 5.

51
00:09:50,155 --> 00:09:59,812
These are three different numbers, because each b is at least 1. The smallest is at least 1, and the largest is at most n. So the set is in A sub n.

52
00:10:00,262 --> 00:10:14,704
Apply f to this set. The differences give back b 1, b 2 and b 3. So the two maps are inverses, and by Proposition 1.11 f is a bijection. The question does not ask for this check.

53
00:10:15,154 --> 00:10:29,133
Here is the written solution. First the definition: for S equal to a, b, c with a less than b less than c, f of S is a, b minus a, c minus b. Say that the elements are in increasing order.

54
00:10:29,583 --> 00:10:33,019
Then the table for A sub 5, with all 10 lines.

55
00:10:33,469 --> 00:10:43,964
Then the check. a is at least 1. b minus a and c minus b are at least 1, because the elements are increasing integers. The sum is c, which is at most n.

56
00:10:44,414 --> 00:10:50,637
Last, the inverse: b 1, b 1 plus b 2, and b 1 plus b 2 plus b 3.

57
00:10:51,087 --> 00:10:59,632
Problem 3 is worth 8 marks. We count compositions of n. The number of parts is 1 mod 5, and each part is 2 or 3 mod 5.

58
00:11:00,082 --> 00:11:20,841
For example, 3 plus 2 plus 2 plus 2 plus 2 plus 2 is a composition of 13. It has 6 parts, and 6 is 1 mod 5. Each part is 2 or 3. So it counts. The composition 13, with one part, also counts. 13 is 3 mod 5.

59
00:11:21,291 --> 00:11:36,848
2 plus 2 plus 2 plus 2 plus 2 does not count. It has 5 parts, and 5 is 0 mod 5. The composition 4 plus 5 does not count either, because 4 and 5 are not allowed parts.

60
00:11:37,298 --> 00:11:53,042
The answer can be a coefficient of a series with no infinite sum. Define the set and the weight function, and cite the results from class. Pause the video and try it. A common mistake is phi of P over 1 minus phi of P. That is the String Lemma, and it allows any number of parts.

61
00:11:53,492 --> 00:12:07,470
Start with one part. Let P be the set of positive integers that are 2 or 3 mod 5. So P is 2, 3, 7, 8, 12, 13, and so on. The blue cells are in P.

62
00:12:07,920 --> 00:12:22,131
The weight of a part is its value. So the generating series of P has a 1 at x to the p for each p in P. It is x squared plus x cubed plus x to the 7 plus x to the 8, and so on.

63
00:12:22,581 --> 00:12:28,478
Group the terms in pairs. Each pair is x squared plus x cubed, times a power of x to the 5.

64
00:12:28,928 --> 00:12:46,483
So phi of P is x squared plus x cubed, times 1 plus x to the 5 plus x to the 10, and so on. By the geometric series, phi of P equals x squared plus x cubed, over 1 minus x to the 5. This has no infinite sum.

65
00:12:46,933 --> 00:12:58,256
A composition with k parts, all in P, is a k-tuple of elements of P. So the compositions with k parts form the set P to the k, the Cartesian product of k copies of P.

66
00:12:58,706 --> 00:13:10,456
The weight of a composition is the sum of its parts. This is the n of the question. By the Product Lemma, the generating series of P to the k is phi of P, to the power k.

67
00:13:10,906 --> 00:13:27,903
The number of parts must be 1, 6, 11, and so on. That is, k equals 5 j plus 1 for some j at least 0. So the set S is the union of the sets P to the 5 j plus 1. They are disjoint, because their elements have different numbers of parts.

68
00:13:28,353 --> 00:13:35,551
By the Sum Lemma, in its infinite form, phi of S is the sum over j of phi of P to the 5 j plus 1.

69
00:13:36,001 --> 00:13:46,543
Phi of P has constant term 0. So we can use the geometric series with phi of P to the 5 in place of x. The result is phi of P, over 1 minus phi of P to the 5.

70
00:13:46,993 --> 00:14:01,203
Put the two parts together. The number of compositions is the coefficient of x to the n in phi of P over 1 minus phi of P to the 5. Here phi of P is x squared plus x cubed, over 1 minus x to the 5.

71
00:14:01,653 --> 00:14:14,006
You can also clear the inner fractions. Multiply the top and the bottom by 1 minus x to the 5, to the power 5. The result is the second form. The question does not ask for it.

72
00:14:14,456 --> 00:14:22,630
Check small n. A computer expands the series. The coefficients are 1 at x squared, x cubed, x to the 7 and x to the 8.

73
00:14:23,080 --> 00:14:36,826
At n equal to 12 the coefficient is 2: the composition 12, and six 2s. At n equal to 13 it is 7: the composition 13, and the 6 orders of five 2s and one 3.

74
00:14:37,276 --> 00:14:47,261
At n equal to 10 the coefficient is 0. Five 2s have the wrong number of parts. A brute-force count of all compositions up to n equal to 20 gives the same numbers.

75
00:14:47,711 --> 00:14:54,212
Here is the written solution. Define P and its weight, and find phi of P with the geometric series.

76
00:14:54,662 --> 00:15:05,158
Define the set S of compositions as a union, and give its weight function: the sum of the parts. Say that the answer is the coefficient of x to the n in phi of S.

77
00:15:05,608 --> 00:15:11,365
Name the Product Lemma for P to the k. Say why the sets are disjoint, then name the Sum Lemma.

78
00:15:11,815 --> 00:15:18,364
Last, use the geometric series. Say that phi of P has constant term 0. Then write the answer.

79
00:15:18,814 --> 00:15:31,213
Problem 4 is worth 5 marks. S sub n is the set of non-empty subsets of 1 to n. There are two weights. w of X is the smallest element of X. v of X is the largest element of X.

80
00:15:31,663 --> 00:15:41,090
Part a asks for the generating series of S sub 3. S sub 3 has 7 subsets. Here they are, one in each row. Pause the video and try it.

81
00:15:41,540 --> 00:15:54,218
Write the smallest element of each set. Four subsets contain 1, so they have weight 1. Two subsets, 2 and 2 3, have weight 2. One subset, 3, has weight 3.

82
00:15:54,668 --> 00:16:00,380
So phi of S sub 3, with respect to w, is 4x plus 2x squared plus x cubed.

83
00:16:00,830 --> 00:16:15,923
Now the largest element. Only the subset 1 has weight 1. 2 and 1 2 have weight 2. Four subsets contain 3, so they have weight 3. With respect to v, the series is x plus 2x squared plus 4x cubed.

84
00:16:16,373 --> 00:16:31,699
Part b replaces S sub n with T, the set of all finite subsets of N. The empty set has no smallest or largest element. So we use the non-empty finite subsets, as in part a. In the notes, N is 0, 1, 2, and so on.

85
00:16:32,149 --> 00:16:44,827
The generating series is a formal power series only if each weight has finitely many elements. Then the coefficient of x to the n is a number. This is the condition in Definition 2.5, the weight function.

86
00:16:45,277 --> 00:17:02,367
Take w, the smallest element. The sets 1; 1 2; 1 3; 1 4; and so on, all have weight 1. There are infinitely many of them. So the coefficient of x is not defined. With respect to w, the series is not a formal power series.

87
00:17:02,817 --> 00:17:17,817
Take v, the largest element. If v of X equals m, then X contains m, and the other elements form a subset of 0 to m minus 1. There are 2 to the m such subsets. For m equal to 3, there are 8.

88
00:17:18,267 --> 00:17:33,035
So each weight has finitely many sets, and the series is a formal power series. It is the sum of 2 to the m, x to the m, which is 1 over 1 minus 2x. If N starts at 1, the series is x over 1 minus 2x.

89
00:17:33,485 --> 00:17:47,277
Problem 5 is worth 4 marks. S is the set 0, 1, up to 50. The weight of a is 4a plus 3. Find the generating series, and write it as a simplified rational expression. Pause the video and try it.

90
00:17:47,727 --> 00:17:56,690
The element 0 has weight 3. The element 1 has weight 7. The element 2 has weight 11. Each step adds 4 to the weight.

91
00:17:57,140 --> 00:18:10,050
So the series has a 1 at x cubed, x to the 7, x to the 11, and so on. All other coefficients are 0. The last element, 50, has weight 4 times 50 plus 3, which is 203.

92
00:18:10,500 --> 00:18:24,618
Phi of S is the sum over a from 0 to 50 of x to the 4a plus 3. Take out x cubed. The rest is the sum of x to the 4, to the power a. This is a finite geometric sum with 51 terms.

93
00:18:25,068 --> 00:18:34,681
Multiply phi of S by x to the 4. Each term moves 4 places to the right. The terms now sit at x to the 7, up to x to the 207.

94
00:18:35,131 --> 00:18:47,716
Subtract the second row from the first. Each power from x to the 7 to x to the 203 is in both rows, so it cancels. Only x cubed and minus x to the 207 remain.

95
00:18:48,166 --> 00:18:55,271
So 1 minus x to the 4, times phi of S, equals x cubed minus x to the 207.

96
00:18:55,721 --> 00:19:10,303
Divide by 1 minus x to the 4. Phi of S equals x cubed times 1 minus x to the 204, over 1 minus x to the 4. The exponent 204 is 4 times 51: the step times the number of terms.

97
00:19:10,753 --> 00:19:20,413
A common mistake is x to the 200 in the numerator. Count the terms: a goes from 0 to 50, so there are 51 terms, not 50.

98
00:19:20,863 --> 00:19:33,866
To recap. Problem 1: count S in two ways, and split the second count by the size of A intersect B. Problem 2: the bijection takes the gaps between the elements, and the inverse takes partial sums.

99
00:19:34,316 --> 00:19:51,917
Problem 3: the compositions are a disjoint union of powers of P. Use the Product and Sum Lemmas, then the geometric series. Problem 4: check that each weight has finitely many elements. Problem 5: a finite geometric sum with 51 terms.

100
00:19:52,367 --> 00:19:55,711
Video 18b continues with problems 6 to 10.
